{
  "note": [
    "The Helipad's fabric (DR-113), cut from the one tower layout in scripts/fabric-tower.mjs -- regenerate from there, do not hand-edit. HELIPAD.metadata.json v3. The fence is what sizes the room: from 500 m the horizon is 0.72 degrees BELOW level, so \"your view is limited to cloud-filled sky\" (compone.zil 2745-2746) is a measurable claim, and a fence 2.5 m tall on a ring of radius 16 blocks everything under 3.2 degrees above a 1.6 m eye. Solid panels to 1.2 m, heavy mesh over them, one continuous run, no gate. The deck is drawn as a 32 m plate rather than a disc because the stairhead has to be a hole and the kit's drum lays a solid floor; the fence hides the plate's corners from every eye that can see it.",
    "The stairhead is a 3 m hole in the deck 4.4 m due west of the eye, with a kerb and a rail round it, and under it the shaft: 25.68 m of concrete and plate round a spiral that turns eight times down to the Tower Core's ceiling, which is where the core's own stair takes over. UP costs 30 time units and DOWN 15 (compone.zil 2731, 2749), which is what says the shaft is tall; nothing of the core is really in sight down it, and the warm glow at the bottom is the core's lamps, painted, not a fixture here. The pad's centre stands 2.2 m east of the tower's axis: the core puts its stair at x -2.2 and this room puts its stairhead 4.4 m west of its own eye, and both of those are settled.",
    "East, the craft: the cabin is HELICOPTER's own shell and its 0.4 m flank is the fuselage's skin, 5.6 m across and 14.4 m nose to tail with the nose north, standing on gear that holds the cabin floor 1.6 m over the deck. Four blades set fore, aft, port and starboard with tips at [10,-7], [16,-1], [10,5] and [4,-1], which keeps every one of them between bearings 55 and 117 and clear of the seams at 45 and 135. The side doors stand open on the west flank and the cargo bay shows through them. No sea, no island, no tower below, no pad lighting, no marking and no second craft: outside the fence nothing exists."
  ],
